The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
void amdgpu_userq_fence_driver_free(struct amdgpu_usermode_queue *userq)
{
	amdgpu_userq_walk_and_drop_fence_drv(&userq->fence_drv_xa);
	xa_destroy(&userq->fence_drv_xa);
	amdgpu_userq_fence_driver_put(userq->fence_drv);
}
```
Reason: This function destroys the queue but does not call `dma_fence_put(userq->last_fence)` to release the extra reference held by `userq->last_fence`. Consequently, the last fence object remains alive after the queue is destroyed, preventing the kernel slab cache from being emptied during module unload and triggering the BUG.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
void amdgpu_userq_fence_driver_free(struct amdgpu_usermode_queue *userq)
{
	dma_fence_put(userq->last_fence);
	amdgpu_userq_walk_and_drop_fence_drv(&userq->fence_drv_xa);
	xa_destroy(&userq->fence_drv_xa);
	amdgpu_userq_fence_driver_put(userq->fence_drv);
}
```
Reason: Adding `dma_fence_put(userq->last_fence)` drops the extra reference before the fence driver’s xarray is destroyed. This ensures the last fence can be freed properly, so the slab cache has no remaining objects when the module is unloaded.

